{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Deprecate REBAR_LLM_MODEL, add ADR and example configs\n\n## Context / Problem\nOnce classes are the interface, `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` is a second, ambiguous way to say the same thing —\na single scalar that cannot express a per-tier mapping. Keeping both indefinitely reintroduces the\nambiguity this epic removes. It is also load-bearing today: the production cutover's kill switch\n[rebar:eb6e-f43a-e86b-443a] is documented as \"point `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` back at `anthropic:...`\".\n\n## Approach\nDeprecate with a MIGRATION WINDOW, registered via `_scheduled(...)` with the default `remove_in=_MAJOR` (v1.0.0). THIS IS THE PARENT'S DECISION, not a default I chose: eb58 states in bold that \"`REBAR_LLM_MODEL` is DEPRECATED with a migration window rather than kept as a permanent alias\", with the rationale that there are few clients today and it is a single-scalar knob. An earlier draft of this plan switched to `_permanent(...)` on the grounds that all six existing env deprecations in `_deprecations.REGISTRY` use it — a real observation, but the WRONG authority to resolve this against. The parent CONSIDERED the permanent alias and rejected it deliberately, so the six precedents are the repo's general habit and this is a considered exception. Approach and Scope now agree on the window. Within the window `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` is still\nhonoured, emits a deprecation warning ONCE per process, and maps to ALL THREE classes — the\nwidest-compatibility reading, so an operator who set one model keeps getting exactly that model\neverywhere and nothing breaks mid-window.\n\nDeprecation (rather than keeping it as a permanent shorthand) is a deliberate operator-approved call:\nthere are few clients today, and it yields a single unambiguous config model.\n\nBecause the kill switch's current wording goes stale the moment this lands, rewriting it in class\nterms is part of THIS ticket's contract — a kill switch that is wrong during an incident is worse than\nnone.\n\nShips example configs per provider in docs, so the easy path is documentation rather than presets\nshipped in code that clients select by name.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py` — the CALL to `warn_deprecated` only. It must NOT host the class fan-out:\n  `config.py` has no module-level `model_classes` import BY DESIGN (verified: an AST check returns\n  False, and `model_classes` imports `config` at module scope so the reverse closes a cycle).\n- `src/rebar/llm/model_classes.py` — **the map-to-all-three fan-out belongs HERE**, beside the slot\n  resolution it has to compose with: `load_class_slots` (`:207`), `parse_class_slots` (`:166`) and\n  `_DEFAULT_MODEL_BY_CLASS` (`:47`). A bare `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` becomes the default for all three slots\n  at the point slots are built, which is also where the documented per-field precedence\n  (CLI > env > config table > built-in default) is already applied — putting it in `config.py`\n  behind a lazy import would duplicate that precedence logic on the far side of the seam.\n- `src/rebar/_deprecations.py` — **REQUIRED, and previously missing from this Scope.** The\n  deprecation must be REGISTERED here via `_scheduled(...)` in `REGISTRY` (`:62`, `:100`) and emitted\n  through `warn_deprecated` (`:299`), per the parent's migration-window decision. Note this is the\n  MINORITY form for env vars in this registry — the six existing env entries all use `_permanent` —\n  so state in the entry why this one is scheduled, or a later reader will assume it is a mistake. This is ENFORCED, not stylistic:\n  `tests/unit/test_deprecation_registry.py:125::test_no_raw_deprecation_emissions` source-scans all\n  of `src/rebar/` and fails any deprecation signal that does not route through `warn_deprecated`.\n  A hand-rolled `warnings.warn` in `config.py` would fail that test AND be invisible to\n  `scripts/gen_env_registry.py`, which imports `rebar._deprecations.REGISTRY` and unions it into the\n  generated env docs.\n- `docs/adr/` — an ADR recording the class model and the deprecation window.\n- `docs/` — example configs (Anthropic-only, Bedrock-only, mixed-provider, local+fallback).\n- The cutover runbook's kill-switch entry — restated in class terms.\n\n## Testing\nUnit tier for the mapping and the warning; docs assertions for the rest.\n- `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` set and no classes configured: all three classes resolve to that model.\n- the deprecation warning is emitted once, not per call.\n- classes configured AND `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` set: classes win, warning still emitted.\n- with neither set, resolution is unchanged from today's defaults.\n\n## Interaction with S8's kill switch (eb6e) — NOT a safety conflict, but it must be handled\n\nS8 (`eb6e`) documents its production recovery path as \"a documented one-line kill switch\n(`REBAR_LLM_MODEL` back to `anthropic:…`)\", scopes `infra/compose/` and `infra/runbooks/` around it,\nand has an AC to EXECUTE it end-to-end. S8 is blocked behind `eb58`, which cannot close until THIS\nticket does — so the deprecation lands FIRST and S8 would then build a recovery path on a deprecated\nvariable.\n\n**That does not break the kill switch, and this ticket must not be read as breaking it.** VERIFIED:\n`_deprecations._permanent(...)` — the form all six existing env deprecations use, and the form this\nplan adopts — sets `remove_in=None` with `permanent=True`: it WARNS and NEVER removes. So\n`REBAR_LLM_MODEL` keeps working indefinitely. A kill\nswitch that sets it therefore still works, and still works after this change lands.\n\nUPDATE — THE DURABILITY CONCERN DISSOLVES under the permanent-alias decision above: if the alias is\nnever removed, S8's runbook never rots, so expressing the kill switch in class terms becomes a\ncoherence PREFERENCE rather than a durability fix. Originally recorded as: a runbook that names a\ndeprecated variable as THE recovery lever rots when the variable is removed at the next major, and it contradicts the direction this epic exists to\nestablish. So the resolution is documentation, not mechanism — no change to S8's safety behaviour:\n\n- this ticket's docs criterion covers recovery documentation too, so nothing it writes presents the\n  deprecated variable as the way to recover;\n- `eb6e` should express its kill switch in CLASS terms (the class slots, or\n  `REBAR_LLM_<CLASS>_MODEL`), which is a strictly equivalent one-line operator action. Recorded as a\n  comment on `eb6e` rather than edited into it, since redesigning another ticket's production\n  recovery path is the operator's call and not a side effect of a deprecation ticket.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] the four example configs named in Scope exist under `docs/` — Anthropic-only, Bedrock-only,\n      mixed-provider, and local+fallback — and each is a COMPLETE `[tool.rebar.llm.model_classes]`\n      table a reader can paste, not a fragment. Added because Scope listed this deliverable with no\n      criterion covering it, so it could have shipped as prose about configs rather than configs.\n- [ ] a test asserts `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` alone maps to all three classes\n- [ ] a test asserts the deprecation warning is emitted when `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` is set, via\n      `warn_deprecated` (so the registry is the single source of the message).\n      **THE \"ONCE PER PROCESS\" REQUIREMENT IS WITHDRAWN.** It was invented by an earlier draft of\n      this plan and has NO mechanism: `warn_deprecated` (`_deprecations.py:299-329`) emits\n      UNCONDITIONALLY on every call — verified, there is no `_seen` set, no `_emitted` flag and no\n      `warnings.warn` filter — and none of the six existing env deprecations dedupes either. Adding\n      per-process state solely for this one surface would make it the odd one out for no stated\n      benefit. Per-call emission is the established behaviour; match it.\n\n- [ ] a test asserts explicit class config takes precedence over `REBAR_LLM_MODEL`\n- [ ] an ADR exists recording the class model, the deprecation window, and the rejected alternatives\n- [ ] every doc that presents `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` as the way to choose a model presents the class\n      configuration instead, with the env var marked deprecated. RE-MEASURED across the WHOLE repo, because an earlier\n      version of this criterion said \"MEASURED five files\" from a grep scoped to `docs/` and\n      `infra/` only and therefore MISSED THE PROJECT README. There are SIX prose files:\n      `README.md` (line 239 — the front page, presenting `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` as THE documented\n      multi-provider interface, so the most important of the six), `docs/llm-framework.md`,\n      `docs/config.md`, `docs/plan-review-gate.md`, `docs/workflow-authoring-v2.md`, and the\n      generated `docs/env-vars.md` (regenerated, never hand-edited). Retargeted from an earlier version of this criterion that named the cutover\n      runbook's kill-switch entry: VERIFIED VACUOUS — `grep -rn REBAR_LLM_MODEL infra/runbooks/`\n      returns NOTHING, so no runbook ever offered it as a recovery lever and that criterion would\n      have passed without any work. The kill-switch entry itself belongs to S8 (eb6e), which owns\n      `infra/runbooks/`"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785586768932318001,"uuid":"ba0695dc-89b1-48aa-88a9-8fdd9f3cd457"}